Given the following propositions:

(1) Let set $M={x|0 < x\leqslant 3}$, $N={x|0 < x\leqslant 2}$, then "$a\in M$" is a sufficient but not necessary condition for "$a\in N$";

(2) The converse of the proposition "If $a\in M$, then $b\notin M$" is: "If $b\in M$, then $a\notin M$";

(3) If proposition $p\land q$ is false, then $p$ and $q$ are both false propositions;

(4) The negation of proposition $P$: "$\exists {{x}\_{0}}\in R,x\_{0}^{2}-{{x}\_{0}}-1 > 0$" is "$\forall x\in R,{{x}^{2}}-x-1\leqslant 0$"

The number of true propositions among the above is ( ).
A: (1)
B: (2)
C: (3)
D: (4)

In triangle $ABC$, with sides $a$, $b$, and $c$ opposite to angles $A$, $B$, and $C$ respectively, it is given that $a=2b$ and that $\sin A$, $\sin C$, $\sin B$ form an arithmetic sequence.
(1) Find the value of $\cos A$;  
(2) If the area of triangle $ABC$ is $S_{\triangle ABC} = \frac {8 \sqrt {15}}{3}$, find the value of $c$.

Given the functions $f(x)=3-2\log_{2}x$ and $g(x)=\log_{2}x$.

(1) When $x \in [1,4]$, find the range of the function $h(x)=[f(x)+1]\cdot g(x)$;

(2) If for any $x \in [1,4]$, the inequality $f(x^{2})\cdot f(\sqrt{x}) > k\cdot g(x)$ always holds, find the range of the real number $k$.

